{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"blamable-halfwitted-nightjar","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A bug closed as a DUPLICATE withholds its parent's certification forever, with no non-force remedy\n\n## The two halves of the gate disagree\n\n**The close path grants dispositions an exemption.** `_commands/transition_close.py:253-260`:\n\n> A duplicate / not-a-bug / escalated bug with a durable replacement relation is a disposition, not\n> a claim that this ticket's acceptance criteria were implemented. Skip the completion-only checks\n> (including file-impact and the billable verifier) [...]\n\nSo `rebar transition <bug> in_progress closed --class duplicate` returns exit 0 and mints **no**\n`completion-verifier` attestation. That is correct and deliberate: a duplicate never claimed to\nimplement its own ACs, so verifying its completion would be meaningless (and billable).\n\n**The certification path does not honor that exemption.** `llm/completion.py:170-199`\n(`child_closure_findings`) requires a valid `completion-verifier` attestation on **every** closed\ndirect child, with no disposition branch. A disposition-closed child therefore lands in\n`uncertified`, and `gate_ops.py:131`'s `certifiable = not uncertified` withholds the parent's\nsignature.\n\n## Why this is a trap, not merely an inconvenience\n\nThe two rules are each individually right and compose into a state with **no non-force exit**:\n\n* the child cannot earn an attestation — reopening and re-closing takes the same exemption branch\n  every time, so the outcome is identical on every attempt;\n* `--force-close` produces exactly the uncertified state the operator ruling prohibits;\n* the only other escapes are dishonest: pick a close class that is not the true disposition, delete\n  the true `duplicates` link, or de-parent the child so it leaves `children`. Each makes the tree\n  certify by misrepresenting it.\n\n## Live instance, with the substance intact\n\nEpic `e369-a449-4773-48fb` (Jira DC support). Child `0059-c7f0-cce1-4077` is a true duplicate of\n`23ed-de0a-765b-49aa` — the same defect diagnosed to its actual cause, fixed and merged as change\n1124. Measured just now:\n\n```\nchild_closure_findings('e369-a449-4773-48fb')\n  UNCERTIFIED: child 0059 is closed but its completion closure is not certified/valid\n               (signature: unsigned)\nverify_signature('23ed-de0a-765b-49aa', kind='completion-verifier') -> certified\n```\n\n**The replacement is certified AND is a direct sibling under the same epic.** So the work IS attested\ninside this tree; the gate simply will not look through the `duplicates` edge to find it. The epic is\ndenied certification over a bookkeeping gap, not a missing verification.\n\n## Candidate remedy — NOT to be applied without an operator ruling\n\nTeach `child_closure_findings` the same disposition branch the close path already has: when a closed\nchild carries a disposition class (`duplicate` / `not_a_bug` / `escalated`) **and** a net-active\nreplacement link to a live ticket, resolve certification **through** that link — certified iff the\nreplacement's own closure is certified-and-valid — rather than demanding an attestation the child is\nexempt from producing. `_has_live_replacement_link` already implements the predicate, so both halves\ncould share one definition instead of drifting.\n\n**Why this is an operator ruling and not an agent's call:** it changes when a parent close is\ncertifiable, which is gate semantics — the same class of decision as the degrade-open remedy on\n[rebar:1019-e1e9-5117-4795] and [rebar:2a76]. A wrong call here either keeps blocking honest closes\nor lets an unattested subtree certify by pointing at an unrelated ticket. Recording it rather than\nfixing it unilaterally.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the operator rules on whether disposition-closed children resolve certification through their\n      replacement link, or must remain uncertified by design\n- [ ] if the remedy is adopted: `child_closure_findings` and `transition_close._has_live_replacement_link`\n      share ONE definition of \"disposition with a live replacement\", and a test pins that a\n      duplicate-closed child whose replacement is certified does NOT withhold the parent's signature\n- [ ] if the remedy is adopted: a test pins the negative case too — a disposition-closed child whose\n      replacement is itself uncertified DOES still withhold certification\n- [ ] epic e369's certification status is re-derived under the ruling and recorded on that epic","id":"738a-376e-4829-42ca","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Certification path ignores the disposition exemption: a duplicate-closed child withholds its parent's signature with no non-force remedy"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785861707245551001,"uuid":"385eb2db-19d9-4302-a5fd-de0be0127ac8"}